.TH E4DEFRAG 8 "May 2009" "e4defrag version 2.0"
.SH NAME
e4defrag \- online defragmenter for ext4 file system
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B e4defrag
[
.B \-c
]
[
.B \-v[v]
]
[
.B \-\-allow-equal-layout
|
.B \-\-shake
]
.I target
\&...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B e4defrag
reduces fragmentation of extent based file. The file targeted by
.B e4defrag
is created on ext4 file system made with "-O extent" option (see
.BR mke2fs (8)).
The targeted file gets more contiguous blocks and improves the file access
speed.
.PP
.I target
is a regular file, a directory, or a device that is mounted as ext4 file system.
If
.I target
is a directory,
.B e4defrag
reduces fragmentation of all files in it. If
.I target
is a device,
.B e4defrag
gets the mount point of it and reduces fragmentation of all files in this mount
point.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-c
Get a current fragmentation count and an ideal fragmentation count, and
calculate fragmentation score based on them. By seeing this score, we can
determine whether we should execute
.B e4defrag
to
.IR target .
When used with
.B \-v
option, the current fragmentation count and the ideal fragmentation count are
printed for each file.
For a single file,
.B \-c \-v
retains the extent-map dump.
.B \-c \-vv
uses the same statistics output.
.IP
Also this option outputs the average data size in one extent. If you see it,
you'll find the file has ideal extents or not. Note that the maximum extent
size is 131072KB in ext4 file system (if block size is 4KB).
.IP
If this option is specified,
.I target
is never defragmented.
.TP
.B \-v
Print the extent count before and after defragmentation in the familiar
.B "extents: before -> after"
format, retaining bracketed
.B "[ OK ]"
and
.B "[ NG ]"
results.  A successful result can mean that the file required no movement.
Early exclusions retain the filename and reason, such as "File size is 0",
with the upstream-style NG marker; they do not print 100% OK.
.TP
.B \-vv
Keep compact file lines, adding the estimated best extent count in parentheses
and an explicit bracketed action status.  Skipped files show a short reason
instead of a completion percentage.  Each file's result, including errors or
partial movement, stays on one line so filtering by filename retains it.
No per-file placement tables or allocation diagnostics are printed.
.IP
The estimated best count comes from filesystem geometry, falling back to 1
when geometry is unavailable. It does not guarantee an attainable layout,
especially for sparse files. Both verbosity levels collect a final extent
count; detailed placement maps require a compile-time DEBUG build.
.TP
.B \-\-allow-equal-layout
Attempt relocation even when the file already meets the estimated best
extent count.  Accept a donor with no more contiguous data runs than the
source, including an equal count.  This can change physical placement
without reducing fragmentation.
.TP
.B \-\-shake
Attempt relocation of every eligible allocated file, accepting even a
donor with more contiguous data runs than the source.  This can increase
fragmentation of the target file.  It may rearrange free space for later
allocations, but improvement to other files is not guaranteed.
.PP
Without a movement option, files at the estimated best count are skipped
and a donor must have fewer contiguous data runs than the source.
The movement options also permit relocation of single-block files.
They preserve grouped donor allocation and source holes, and do not bypass
ownership, file-lock, free-space, or kernel eligibility checks.
Empty files and files with no allocated blocks remain skipped.
The two movement options are mutually exclusive and cannot be combined with
.BR \-c .
.PP
Donor run counts are the existing candidate-selection heuristic.
Because the donor can include allocation over source holes, accepting a
donor does not guarantee an improvement in the resulting source layout.
Use
.B \-vv
to distinguish actual movement from skipped work, even when the before/after
extent counts are equal.
.SH RESULTS
Normal output and
.B \-v
retain the upstream-style file/progress line and bracketed OK/NG results.
Errors and any known partial movement are reported regardless of verbosity.
With
.B \-vv
these brackets instead distinguish the following actions:
.TP
.B MOVED
The move operation completed and the kernel reported moving blocks.
This does not guarantee a reduced extent count or faster access.
.TP
.B UNCHANGED
The move operation completed but reported moving no blocks.
.TP
.B SKIPPED
No movement was attempted because of an eligibility or policy decision.
The reason appears on the same line, including empty files, locks, and
rejected donors.
.TP
.B FAILED
A required operation failed.  The stage and error are printed even without
.BR \-v .
Any known movement already completed is reported; failure does not undo it.
.PP
The Success/Failure summary excludes non-file directory entries from its
file counts. Normal exclusions are not operational failures, even though
ordinary verbose output retains their upstream NG marker.
Normal policy skips count as successful processing;
.B \-vv
adds the separate action counts.  A failed optional final extent count is a
warning and does not change the outcome of a completed move.
.PP
Final defragmentation result lines are written to standard output, followed
by any human-readable explanations.  Ordinary path spelling is retained;
control bytes in these file/progress lines are escaped.
Startup and traversal diagnostics may also appear on standard error.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Zero means processing completed without operational or traversal errors.
Normal policy skips are not errors.  A nonzero status indicates an invalid
invocation or a failed requested operation, even if other files succeeded.
Optional measurement and cache-advice warnings alone do not cause failure.
.SH NOTES
Progress is updated in place when standard output is a terminal and
.B TERM
is set to a nonempty value other than
.BR dumb .
When output is redirected to a file or pipe, results are printed without
cursor-control sequences or intermediate progress updates.
The movement percentage tracks logical file position, including holes;
it is not a measure of improvement or the fraction of allocated bytes moved.
Set
.B TERM=dumb
to force plain output when recording a terminal session.
.PP
.B e4defrag
does not support swap file, files in lost+found directory, and files allocated
in indirect blocks. When
.I target
is a device or a mount point,
.B e4defrag
doesn't defragment files in mount point of other device.
.PP
It is safe to run e4defrag on a file while it is actively in use by another
application.  Since the contents of file blocks are copied using the
page cache, this can result in a performance slowdown to both e4defrag
and the application due to contention over the system's memory and disk
bandwidth.
.PP
If the file system's free space is fragmented, or if there is
insufficient free space available, e4defrag may not be able
to improve the file's fragmentation.
.PP
Non-privileged users can execute
.B e4defrag
to their own file, but the score is not printed if
.B \-c
option is specified. Therefore, it is desirable to be executed by root user.
.SH AUTHOR
Written by Akira Fujita <a-fujita@rs.jp.nec.com> and Takashi Sato
<t-sato@yk.jp.nec.com>.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mke2fs (8),
.BR mount (8).

